REALROM sample.note d132 [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is this helper's ARGUMENT: the backward scan reached the helper's entry, where the register holds whatever the caller supplied, so the bank cannot be known from inside the helper body. Each CALL SITE is resolved separately; see the call sites' own annotations.
REALROM sample.note d163 [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is RESTORED here, not resolved: this call re-commits the bank READ BACK at d150 from write-through bank shadow 004c -- a RAM cell the switch code keeps in step with every bank it commits, so the byte read is the last bank committed through it, carried on the stack across the call at d156 -- the save/restore idiom. The bank after this call is whatever 004c held at that read, not a fresh value this analyzer failed to pin down. That read sits at the ENTRY of FUN_d136 (nothing switches between its entry and the read), so it is the shadow as the caller left it: each of its call sites' own bank, which those sites already track -- the call passes it through unchanged. A property of the game, not a gap in analysis.
